'''Lake Como''',{{efn|{{langx|it|Lago di Como}} {{IPA|it|ˈlaːɡo di ˈkɔːmo|}}, {{IPA|it|- ˈkoːmo|local}}; {{langx|lmo|label=[[Western Lombard]]|Lagh de Comm}} {{IPA|lmo|ˈlɑː‿dːe ˈkɔm|}}, {{lang|lmo|Cómm}} {{IPA|lmo|- ˈkom|}} or {{lang|lmo|Cùmm}} {{IPA|lmo|- ˈkum|}}.}} also known as '''Lario''',{{efn|{{IPA|it|ˈlaːrjo|lang}}; after the {{langx|la|Larius Lacus}}.}} is a lake of [[glacial lake|glacial origin]] in [[Lombardy]], Italy. It has an area of {{convert|146|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}, making it the third-largest lake in Italy, after [[Lake Garda]] and [[Lake Maggiore]]. At over {{convert|400|m}} deep, it is one of the [[List of lakes by depth|deepest lakes in Europe]]. Its characteristic "Y" shape resulted from the movement of the ancient [[Adda (river)|Adda]] glacier, which was diverted by the mountainous terrain and carved the three branches.

Located at the foot of the [[Alps]], Lake Como has been a popular retreat for aristocracy and the wealthy since Roman times, and a major tourist attraction with many artistic and cultural gems. The homonymous city was named Novum Comum by consul [[Julius Caesar]] in 59 BC. Its shores are dotted with numerous [[Villa|villas]] and palaces, such as [[Villa Olmo]], [[Villa Serbelloni]], and [[Villa Carlotta]], known for their historic architecture and elaborate gardens. The mild, humid climate, influenced by the lake, supports a diverse range of subtropical plants as well as traditional Mediterranean crops like olives. The surrounding mountains host typical Alpine flora and fauna. A significant religious site is the [[Sacro Monte di Ossuccio]], a UNESCO [[World Heritage Site]].

Lake Como's strategic location facilitated trade during the Roman era and subsequent periods, but also made it a site of conflict through the [[Middle Ages]] and [[Renaissance]]. The area became renowned for its [[silk|silk industry]] in the 19th and 20th centuries, contributing to the region's prosperity. Today, the economy relies heavily on tourism, manufacturing, and craftsmanship. However, the lake's popularity has led to significant challenges related to [[overtourism]], impacting local infrastructure, environment, and communities. Lake Como remains an important cultural reference, featuring in literature, film, and music.

[[File:Monte San Primo - lago di Como.jpg|thumb|Lake Como from Monte San Primo]]

==Etymology and nomenclature==
The lake's ancient Latin name was ''Larius Lacus''. [[William Smith (lexicographer)|William Smith]] suggests the name ''Larius'' is derived from a pre-Roman root ''*lar-'' meaning "hollow place".<ref name="SmithLarius">{{cite web |url=https://www.perseus.tufts.edu/hopper/text?doc=Perseus:text:1999.04.0064:entry=larius-lacus-geo |title=Larius Lacus - Dictionary of Greek and Roman Geography |last=Smith |first=William |date=1854 |website=Perseus Digital Library |access-date=2025-04-28}}</ref> ''Lacus'' means "lake".

During the Middle Ages, the name ''Lario'' continued to appear, particularly in official and ecclesiastical documents, sometimes rendered as {{lang|it|Lago di Lario}} in local chronicles.<ref name="LarioComoBoat">{{cite web |url=https://lariocomoboat.it/lago-di-como/lago-di-como-o-di-lario-un-viaggio-tra-storia-e-leggenda/ |title=Lago di Como o di Lario? Un viaggio tra storia e leggenda |website=Lario Como Boat |access-date=2025-04-28 |language=Italian |archive-date=23 May 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240523201531/https://lariocomoboat.it/lago-di-como/lago-di-como-o-di-lario-un-viaggio-tra-storia-e-leggenda/ |url-status=live }}</ref> However, starting in late antiquity, the name linked to the principal city, [[Como]] (Latin: ''Comum''), gradually gained prominence, especially in everyday and commercial use. This alternative name, ''Lacus Comacinus'' or ''Comacenus'' (Lake of Como), is found in sources like the [[Antonine Itinerary]] and works by [[Paul the Deacon]].<ref name="SmithLarius"/><ref name="LarioComoBoat"/>

The Renaissance saw a resurgence of interest in the classical name ''Lario'' among scholars and cartographers, fuelled by the rediscovery of ancient texts. Maps from this era often displayed both {{lang|it|Lago di Lario}} and {{lang|it|Lago di Como}}, reflecting the dual naming convention and a growing appreciation for the region's history.<ref name="LarioComoBoat"/>

Today, while ''Lario'' persists in cultural, literary, and historical references, and survives in the names of several lakeside towns (such as [[Gera Lario]], [[Pognana Lario]], and [[Mandello del Lario]]), the name most commonly used by Italians and recognized internationally, particularly within the tourism sector, is {{lang|it|Lago di Como}}.<ref name="LarioComoBoat"/><ref name="SmithLarius"/>

==Geography and geology==
Lake Como is situated in the [[Lombardy]] region of Northern Italy, at the southern end of the Alps, about 45 km north of [[Milan]]. Geologically, it lies within the Southern [[Alps]], specifically the Lombardic Basin, an area shaped by a complex history of tectonic activity and surface processes.<ref name="Scardia2020">{{cite journal |last1=Scardia |first1=G. |last2=Muttoni |first2=G. |last3=Sciunnach |first3=D. |title=Geological context and evolution of Lake Como basin (Southern Alps, N Italy) |journal=Swiss Journal of Geosciences |date=2020 |volume=113 |issue=1 |pages=12 |doi=10.1186/s00015-020-00361-7 |doi-access=free |issn=1661-8726 |eissn=1661-8734|hdl=20.500.11850/421467 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> The region experienced significant [[rifting]] during the [[Mesozoic Era]] followed by compressional forces during the [[Alpine orogeny]] in the [[Cenozoic Era]], related to the collision of the African and Eurasian plates. This compression phase, particularly activity along major fault systems near the [[Periadriatic Seam]] (like the Insubric Line), influenced the landscape's structure.<ref name="Scardia2020"/> The lake basin is primarily carved into Mesozoic carbonate rocks (limestones and marls such as the [[Moltrasio Formation|Moltrasio Limestone]]) overlying a deeper crystalline basement.<ref name="Scardia2020"/><ref name="AttardoComoGeology">{{cite book |title=Indagine geologico-tecnica di supporto al PRUG |author=Studio Attardo |year=2002 |publisher=Comune di Como |pages=670–718 |language=Italian}}</ref> Later Cenozoic sediments, notably the [[Clastic rock|Gonfolite Group]] (Oligocene-Miocene), represent deposits from large submarine fans fed by rivers eroding the nascent Alps.<ref name="PoliMI_Geology">{{citation |title=Il lago di Como, origine fluviale ma morfologia glaciale |work=ArchaeoGEW - Il WebGIS archeologico della Spina Verde di Como |publisher=Politecnico di Milano (contribution/source) |language=Italian}}</ref>

While often described simply as a [[glacial lake]], Lake Como's basin results from combined [[fluvial]] erosion and deep glacial scouring acting upon a pre-existing, structurally controlled valley network.<ref name="Scardia2020"/> A crucial pre-conditioning event was the [[Messinian salinity crisis]] in the late [[Miocene]] (around 5.96–5.33 million years ago), when the [[Mediterranean Sea]] nearly dried up. This caused Alpine rivers, like the paleo-Adda, to carve extremely deep canyons into the landscape, well below current sea level, as they adjusted to the dramatically lowered base level.<ref name="Scardia2020"/> These canyons were subsequently partially filled with sediments during the [[Zanclean flood]] and [[Pliocene]] epoch, but the deeply incised valley system persisted.<ref name="Scardia2020"/>

During the [[Quaternary Period]]'s repeated glaciations, large glaciers originating mainly from the Adda valley flowed southwards, following and intensely modifying this pre-existing river valley system.<ref name="PoliMI_Geology"/><ref name="Scardia2020"/> These powerful glaciers significantly widened the valleys into a characteristic [[U-shaped valley|U-shape]] and eroded the basin much deeper than the previous river canyons, creating the lake's remarkable depth, over {{convert|400|m}}, and overdeepening—its bottom lies over {{convert|200|m}} below sea level.<ref name="Scardia2020"/>

[[File:Aerial photographs 2010-by-RaBoe-47.jpg|thumb|Aerial photograph showing Lake Como's characteristic three-branched shape, with the Alps to the north]]
Lake Como's distinctive shape, resembling an inverted letter "[[Y]]", is a direct result of this geological history. The main glacial flow followed the path of the pre-existing river valley down the northern arm and the southwestern (Como) arm. Glacial erosion eventually breached the mountainous barrier between [[Bellagio (Italian region)|Bellagio]] and [[Varenna]], diverting the main ice flow (and subsequently the Adda river) into the pre-existing valley forming the southeastern branch.<ref name="PoliMI_Geology"/><ref name="Scardia2020"/> The promontory of Bellagio separates the two southern branches at this confluence point, shaped by differential glacial erosion.<ref name="Scardia2020"/> As the glaciers retreated during the [[Last Glacial Maximum]] and subsequent phases, they deposited large [[moraine]] systems, particularly south of the lake, forming the hilly landscape of the [[Brianza]] region.<ref name="PoliMI_Geology"/><ref name="AttardoComoGeology"/>
The mountainous region between the Como and Lecco branches (between the cities of Como, Bellagio, and Lecco) is known as the Larian Triangle ({{lang|it|Triangolo lariano}}).{{efn|The meaning of ''Larian'' is related to Lake Como, from the Latin name ''Larius''.}} It is the source of the [[Lambro]] river. [[Canzo]] is the chief town of the local ''[[Mountain community in Italy|Comunità Montana del Triangolo Lariano]]''.

===Hydrology===
The primary inflow for Lake Como is the [[Adda (river)|Adda River]], which enters the lake near [[Colico]] on the northern branch, having been diverted from its pre-glacial course by glacial action.<ref name="PoliMI_Geology"/> The [[Mera (Lake Como)|Mera River]] also forms a major inflow at the northern end. The Adda is also the sole major outflow, exiting from the southeastern branch.<ref name="Scardia2020"/> This hydrological arrangement leaves the southwestern branch as a cul-de-sac with no major outlets, making the city of Como historically more susceptible to flooding than Lecco.<ref name="Scardia2020"/>

The lake's water balance is dominated by these river flows, with precipitation on the lake surface and evaporation playing secondary roles.<ref name="Galia2021">{{cite journal |last1=Galia |first1=T. |last2=Acquaotta |first2=F. |last3=Fratianni |first3=S. |last4=Gazzena |first4=L. |last5=Negri |first5=S. |title=Water Temperature Trends and Surface Heat Fluxes in Lake Como (Northern Italy) |journal=Climate |volume=9 |issue=1 |pages=8 |date=2021 |doi=10.3390/cli9010008 |doi-access=free |issn=2225-1154 |hdl=11311/1179641 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> With a large volume of approximately {{convert|22.5|km3}}, Lake Como has a [[Lake retention time|residence time]] estimated at 5.5 years.<ref name=iii/> Lake Como is a deep lake exhibiting strong seasonal thermal stratification. It is classified as warm [[Monomictic lake|monomictic]], meaning its waters typically mix fully from surface to bottom only once per year, during late winter (usually February-March) when surface waters cool sufficiently to sink.<ref name="Galia2021"/> This annual mixing is crucial for transporting oxygen to the deep waters. During summer, the lake develops a distinct warm surface layer ([[epilimnion]]), separated from the cold, deep [[hypolimnion]] (where temperatures remain around 6–7 °C year-round) by a sharp transition zone known as the [[thermocline]] (or metalimnion).<ref name="Galia2021"/> Water circulation within the lake is influenced significantly by [[Wind#Local_winds|local winds]], particularly the regular diurnal breezes: the {{lang|it|Breva}} blowing from the south in the afternoon and the {{lang|it|Tivano}} from the north in the morning, which drive surface currents.<ref name="Galia2021"/> Water levels fluctuate seasonally depending on inflows from snowmelt and rainfall, and are regulated by the dam at [[Olginate]] on the Adda river outflow.<ref name="Galia2021"/>

==Ecology==

===Flora===
The mild, humid climate influenced by the lake supports a rich diversity of plant life characteristic of the [[Insubria]] region.<ref name="CMVLC_FloraFauna">{{cite web |url=http://www.cmalpilepontine.it/cmvlarcer/zf/index.php/servizi-aggiuntivi/index/index/idtesto/12 |title=Flora e Fauna |website=Comunità Montana Valli del Lario e del Ceresio |access-date=2025-04-28 |language=Italian |archive-date=15 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230715101923/http://www.cmalpilepontine.it/cmvlarcer/zf/index.php/servizi-aggiuntivi/index/index/idtesto/12 |url-status=live }}</ref> Along the immediate shores and lower slopes, [[Mediterranean climate|Mediterranean]] species like [[Olea europaea|olive trees]] (historically cultivated, evidenced by names like Oliveto Lario), [[Laurus nobilis|laurel]], [[Myrtus|myrtle]], and [[Cupressus|cypress]] thrive. The villa gardens famously cultivate exotic subtropical plants alongside native species.<ref name=CMVLC_FloraFauna/><ref name="ImpellusoGardens"/> Vegetation is strongly zoned by altitude: foothills (up to 800 m) are dominated by mixed deciduous forests of [[Quercus|oak]] (including [[Quercus pubescens|downy oak]] on drier slopes), [[Castanea sativa|chestnut]], [[Carpinus|hornbeam]], [[Fraxinus ornus|manna ash]], and [[Populus|poplar]]. Higher elevations transition to [[Fagus sylvatica|beech]] forests and then extensive [[conifer]] forests (including [[Abies alba|silver fir]], [[Picea abies|spruce]], [[Larix|larch]], and [[Pinus mugo|mountain pine]]). The highest altitudes feature alpine meadows and shrublands with [[Juniperus|juniper]], [[Rhododendron]], [[Vaccinium myrtillus|bilberry]], and [[Alnus alnobetula|green alder]].<ref name=CMVLC_FloraFauna/> The area hosts several [[Endemism|endemic]] plant species, particularly on limestone substrates, such as ''[[Campanula raineri]]'' and ''[[Primula|Primula glaucescens]]''.<ref name=CMVLC_FloraFauna/> True [[aquatic plant|aquatic vegetation]] is relatively scarce due to the steep, deep nature of the shores, mostly confined to shallower areas like the northern {{lang|it|[[Riserva Naturale Pian di Spagna e Lago di Mezzola|Piano di Spagna]]}} nature reserve.<ref name=CMVLC_FloraFauna/>

===Fauna===
The varied habitats support diverse fauna. Larger mammals in the surrounding mountains and forests include [[red deer]], [[roe deer]], and [[chamois]], along with [[wild boar]] and [[Red fox|red foxes]].<ref name=CMVLC_FloraFauna/> [[Avifauna|Birdlife]] is abundant, featuring common forest species and birds of prey like the [[common buzzard]] and [[hawks]]. The lake itself is a vital habitat for [[waterfowl]], especially during migration and wintering. Numerous species of [[Anatidae|ducks]], [[Podicipedidae|grebes]], [[Cygnus (bird)|swans]], [[Laridae|gulls]], and [[Phalacrocoracidae|cormorants]] are present. [[Ardeidae|Herons]], particularly the [[grey heron]], are common residents, favouring quieter stretches of shoreline.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.quicomo.it/social/lago-di-como-regno-aironi.html |title=Lago di Como, il regno degli aironi |website=QuiComo |access-date=2025-04-28 |language=Italian}}</ref>

Lake Como hosts over 25 fish species.<ref name=NicolarattiFauna>{{cite web |url=http://web.tiscali.it/nicolaratti/embolo/fauna.htm |title=Fauna del Lago di Como |website=web.tiscali.it/nicolaratti |access-date=2025-04-28 |language=Italian |archive-date=20 February 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200220222421/http://web.tiscali.it/nicolaratti/embolo/fauna.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> Due to its great depth and limited shallow [[littoral zone]]s, [[pelagic zone|pelagic]] species are dominant. These include the native [[Coregonus|whitefishes]] – locally known as {{lang|it|[[Coregonus lavaretus|lavarello]]}} (common whitefish) and {{lang|it|bondella}} ([[Coregonus wartmanni|blue whitefish]]) – and the shad {{lang|it|[[Alosa agone|agone]]}}. These fish, feeding primarily on [[zooplankton]], form the basis of the lake's traditional commercial fishery and local cuisine (e.g., {{lang|it|[[Alosa agone|missoltino]]}}).{{cn|date=August 2025}} [[Trout]] species also inhabit the deeper waters. The more limited littoral zones support populations of [[Perca fluviatilis|European perch]], [[Esox|northern pike]] (less common due to scarcity of vegetated shallows), [[Tinca tinca|tench]], [[Squalius cephalus|chub]], [[Rutilus rutilus|roach]], and [[Cyprinus carpio|common carp]].<ref name=NicolarattiFauna/> The [[burbot]] (''Lota lota'') is a native bottom-dwelling predator.<ref name=NicolarattiFauna/> Several [[introduced species]] have established populations, some becoming invasive. These include the [[Lepomis gibbosus|pumpkinseed sunfish]], large predatory catfish like the [[Silurus glanis|wels catfish]], and invertebrates such as the [[Dreissena polymorpha|zebra mussel]] and the aggressive [[Procambarus clarkii|red swamp crayfish]], which pose ecological challenges.<ref name=NicolarattiFauna/>

==Climate==
{{see also|Italian Lakes#Climate}}

According to the [[Köppen climate classification]], Lake Como experiences a [[humid subtropical climate]].<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Rubel |first1=Franz |last2=Kottek |first2=Markus |last3=Grieser |first3=Jürgen |last4=Beck |first4=Christian |last5=Rudolf |first5=Bart |last6=Mieczkowski |first6=Stanley |title=Köppen-Geiger climate classification revised: Present and future climates |journal=Meteorologische Zeitschrift |volume=15 |issue=3 |date=June 2006 |pages=259–286 |doi=10.1127/0941-2948/2006/0507 |doi-broken-date=1 July 2025 |issn=0941-2948 |eissn=1610-1227}}</ref> The average temperature in the surrounding region fluctuates from {{cvt|4|C|0}} in January to {{cvt|25|C|0}} in July.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://weatherspark.com/y/62658/Average-Weather-in-Como-Italy-Year-Round|publisher=weatherspark.com|title=Average Weather in Como|access-date=13 March 2024|archive-date=13 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313045554/https://weatherspark.com/y/62658/Average-Weather-in-Como-Italy-Year-Round|url-status=live}}</ref> The water temperature reaches an average of {{cvt|26|C|0}} in July, making it pleasant for water activities during the summer months.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://seatemperature.info/july/lake-como-water-temperature.html|title=Water temperature in Lake Como in July|publisher=Seatemperature.info |access-date=March 13, 2024}}</ref>

The climate around Lake Como features distinct seasonal variations. During winter, the lake exerts a moderating influence, helping to maintain a mild average temperature of {{cvt|7|C}} in the nearby area.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://weatherspark.com/h/s/62658/2023/3/Historical-Weather-Winter-2023-in-Como-Italy#Figures-Temperature|publisher=weatherspark.com|title=Historical Weather in Winter 2023 in Como|access-date=13 March 2024|archive-date=13 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313062222/https://weatherspark.com/h/s/62658/2023/3/Historical-Weather-Winter-2023-in-Como-Italy#Figures-Temperature|url-status=live}}</ref> Snowfall is erratic and generally confined to higher elevations, while occasional periods of frost can occur due to the influence of the [[Siberian High|Siberian Anticyclone]].

Spring and autumn are typically mild and agreeable transitional seasons, with average daytime temperatures around {{cvt|17|C}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://weatherspark.com/h/s/62658/2023/0/Historical-Weather-Spring-2023-in-Como-Italy#Figures-Temperature|publisher=weatherspark.com|title=Historical Weather in Spring 2023 in Como}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://weatherspark.com/h/s/62658/2023/2/Historical-Weather-Fall-2023-in-Como-Italy#Figures-Temperature|publisher=weatherspark.com|title=Historical Weather in Fall 2023 in Como}}</ref> Precipitation tends to be more frequent during these periods.

Summer days are generally warm, with average daytime temperatures exceeding {{cvt|27|C}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://weatherspark.com/h/s/62658/2023/1/Historical-Weather-Summer-2023-in-Como-Italy#Figures-Temperature|publisher=weatherspark.com|title=Historical Weather in Summer 2023 in Como|access-date=13 March 2024|archive-date=13 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313062220/https://weatherspark.com/h/s/62658/2023/1/Historical-Weather-Summer-2023-in-Como-Italy#Figures-Temperature|url-status=live}}</ref> [[2024 European heatwaves|Heatwaves]] have become more common in recent years, occasionally bringing temperatures as high as {{cvt|38|C}} and leading to hot, humid conditions. This season is also subject to thunderstorms and, at times, violent [[hail]]storms.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.quicomo.it/cronaca/como-provincia-nubifragio-allagamenti-grandine-foto-.html|title=Nubifragio, raffiche di vento a 100 km/h, grandine come palline da golf|publisher=quicomo.it |access-date=March 6, 2024|language=it}}</ref>

===Impact of climate change===

Historically, winters in the Lake Como region were characterized by significant snowfall and cold temperatures, with average daily temperatures in January and February often falling below freezing.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://weatherspark.com/h/m/62658/1987/2/Historical-Weather-in-February-1987-in-Como-Italy#Figures-Temperature|publisher=weatherspark.com|title=Historical Weather in February 1987 in Como|access-date=13 March 2024|archive-date=13 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313050820/https://weatherspark.com/h/m/62658/1987/2/Historical-Weather-in-February-1987-in-Como-Italy#Figures-Temperature|url-status=live}}</ref> However, due to [[global warming]], average winter temperatures have progressively increased since the beginning of the 21st century. A record high of {{cvt|21|C}} was recorded on January 27, 2024,<ref>{{cite web|url=https://weatherspark.com/h/d/148114/2024/1/27/Historical-Weather-on-Saturday-January-27-2024-at-Milano-Malpensa-Airport-Italy#Figures-Temperature|publisher=weatherspark.com|title=Historical Weather|access-date=13 March 2024|archive-date=13 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313042717/https://weatherspark.com/h/d/148114/2024/1/27/Historical-Weather-on-Saturday-January-27-2024-at-Milano-Malpensa-Airport-Italy#Figures-Temperature|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.informazione.it/a/3BEAEF18-4306-44A2-80D5-F9566BA80A18/Caldo-Natale-sul-lago-di-Como-il-termometro-sfiora-i-20-gradi-colpa-del-Foehn|title=Caldo Natale sul lago di Como, il termometro sfiora i 20 gradi: colpa del Foehn|date=25 December 2023 |publisher=informazione.it|access-date=March 6, 2024|language=it|archive-date=13 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313045555/https://www.informazione.it/a/3BEAEF18-4306-44A2-80D5-F9566BA80A18/Caldo-Natale-sul-lago-di-Como-il-termometro-sfiora-i-20-gradi-colpa-del-Foehn|url-status=live}}</ref> reflecting this warming trend. Similarly, summers, once known for their pleasant climate, have experienced [[Climate change in Italy|rising average temperatures]] since the 2010s. Severe weather events, such as intense rainstorms and hailstorms, previously infrequent, have become increasingly common, impacting the region's climate patterns.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://edition.cnn.com/2023/10/31/climate/italy-floods-milan-lake-como-venice-climate-intl/index.html|publisher=CNN|title=Lake Como bursts its banks as violent storm pounds Northern Italy|date=31 October 2023|access-date=13 March 2024|archive-date=13 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313072620/https://edition.cnn.com/2023/10/31/climate/italy-floods-milan-lake-como-venice-climate-intl/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://apnews.com/article/europe-business-environment-and-nature-storms-c45405d3845b046436dcf4b8b6ea00ea|publisher=Associated Press|title=Lake Como towns hit by extreme weather|date=27 July 2021|access-date=13 March 2024|archive-date=13 March 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313073327/https://apnews.com/article/europe-business-environment-and-nature-storms-c45405d3845b046436dcf4b8b6ea00ea|url-status=live}}</ref>

